WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:05.390
You are probably familiar with magnetic resonance
imaging machines, either through the claustrophobic

00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:10.530
sensation of being inside one or from seeing
the incredibly high detail cross sections

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:12.780
of the human body they provide.

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:13.780
.

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:17.220
MRIs have completely changed how we view and
understand our bodies.

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:21.270
It has never been easier to visualize organs
with such high details.

00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:27.060
We can safely locate and identify tumors in
the kidneys, brain, stomach, and pancreas.

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:31.840
We can inject paramagnetic contrast agents
into the bloodstream to locate blockages in

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:37.040
the heart, allowing doctors to accurately
implant life-saving stents to open blood vessels.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:42.540
A tiny keyhole surgery that patients can recover
from quickly, where once dangerous open heart

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:45.680
surgery was the only option.

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:50.770
Magnetic resonance imaging has truly changed
the nature of medical diagnosis and treatment.

00:00:50.770 --> 00:00:55.410
A technology that seems straight out of a
sci-fi novel, how they work is a complete

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:57.050
mystery to most.

00:00:57.050 --> 00:01:02.699
To unlock the power of this machine physicists
and engineers first had to discover and master

00:01:02.699 --> 00:01:13.040
the principles of quantum mechanics, superconducting
magnets, computer science, and mathematics.

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:18.530
The clean futuristic white facade of an MRI
machine hides a world of complicated and marvelous

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:21.570
engineering that you may not have ever considered.

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:26.490
Prior to their introduction to medicine, we
peered into our bodies using harmful ionizing

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:29.500
x-rays or low-detailed ultrasounds.

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:34.590
While incredibly useful, even to this day,
these two imaging techniques pale in performance

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:37.950
to the safe, millimeter resolution of MRIs.

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:43.759
Capable of creating a 3D reconstruction of
the body rather than a flat 2D image.

00:01:43.759 --> 00:01:47.880
Achieving all of that without any moving parts.

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:52.520
Imaging in medicine relies on collecting signals
from the body based on the innate physical

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:54.280
properties of tissues.

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:59.409
Ultrasound imaging relies on how sound waves
bounce off tissues of different densities.

00:01:59.409 --> 00:02:04.490
X-rays form images based on the absorption
of high-energy radiofrequency waves.

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:09.310
Magnetic resonance imaging, however, relies
on something far less intuitive, the quantum

00:02:09.310 --> 00:02:11.420
properties of the hydrogen atom.

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:14.250
The human body is teeming with hydrogen atoms

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:17.620
located in water, carbohydrates, and proteins.

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:23.140
To image the body, MRI machines exploit a
quantum property of these hydrogen atoms.

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:25.260
A property called spin.

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:29.670
How it does this is a marvel of modern physics
and engineering.

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:34.360
Spin is an innate property of particles just
like mass and charge.

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:37.720
Spin makes particles behave like tiny bar
magnets.

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:41.550
The proton inside the hydrogen nucleus behaves
like a magnet.

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:46.180
The orientation of its magnetic north is described
probabilistically.

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:50.720
Under normal circumstances, this probability
is evenly distributed.

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:55.810
This means that the combined magnetic field
of many hydrogen atoms cancels out.

00:02:55.810 --> 00:03:01.910
But, this changes when the hydrogens are inserted
into a large external magnetic field just

00:03:01.910 --> 00:03:04.280
like the ones MRIs create.

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:09.530
This changes the distribution of the tiny
magnets, augmenting the number of atoms aligned

00:03:09.530 --> 00:03:12.310
with the external magnetic field.

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:18.099
This imbalance is the source of the MRI signal,
as we can manipulate these tiny magnets to

00:03:18.099 --> 00:03:21.980
produce signals that can be processed into
images.[1]

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:27.209
Once the atoms are aligned in the machine's
incredibly strong magnetic field the machines

00:03:27.209 --> 00:03:31.860
give them a tiny nudge using a magnetic radiofrequency
pulse.

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:37.520
This pulse comes from this set of coils inside
the machine that send pulses by simply applying

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:42.700
an alternating current through its coils at
a very specific frequency.

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:48.129
This nudge misaligns these hydrogen bar magnets
to shift their magnetic field perpendicular

00:03:48.129 --> 00:03:51.790
to the large magnetic field the machine is
creating.

00:03:51.790 --> 00:03:57.659
Naturally, the spins want to align their orientation
back to their original position, aligned with

00:03:57.659 --> 00:04:03.700
the machine's magnetic field, but they don’t
fall back immeditely, they decay in a spiraling

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:04.700
motion.

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:10.709
This decay causes a changing magnetic field,
and by placing a coil of wire nearby, this

00:04:10.709 --> 00:04:15.810
changing magnetic field can induce currents
that can be read as a clear signal.

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:20.640
MRI machines can use the same coils to send
the “nudge pulses” and to read the signal

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:21.970
from the body.

00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:27.419
These coils are placed here, as close to the
patient as possible but still Inside the MRI

00:04:27.419 --> 00:04:28.419
tube.

00:04:28.419 --> 00:04:33.410
For higher contrast and resolution, some machines
use separate coils to transmit pulses and

00:04:33.410 --> 00:04:35.060
receive the signal.

00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:40.350
This allows the receiver coils like these
to be placed much closer to the body, maximizing

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:41.920
the strength of the signal.

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:42.920
[2]

00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:47.690
We can increase the number of hydrogens, aligning
with the external magnetic field by increasing

00:04:47.690 --> 00:04:50.470
the strength of the external magnetic field.

00:04:50.470 --> 00:04:56.020
In turn, the hydrogen magnet can induce a
larger current as it spirals back.

00:04:56.020 --> 00:05:01.810
So, by simply increasing the strength of the
MRIs field, we increase the strength of the

00:05:01.810 --> 00:05:06.150
signal collected and therefore improve image
quality.

00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:12.510
Common MRI field strengths are 1.5 to 3 tesla,
around 300,000 times stronger than the earth's

00:05:12.510 --> 00:05:18.400
magnetic field and 30,000 times stronger than
your common fridge magnet. [3] You absolutely

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:24.039
do not want anything ferrous around these
machines during operation, a field this strong

00:05:24.039 --> 00:05:27.190
can lift nearby wheelchairs straight off the
ground.

00:05:27.190 --> 00:05:33.810
For research purposes, MRIS can produce even
higher magnetic fields, up to 20 teslas.

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:37.910
Achieving this intense magnetic field comes
as no easy feat.

00:05:37.910 --> 00:05:42.880
Early MRIs used permanent magnets as their
source of the main magnetic field but only

00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:48.410
reached strengths of 0.5T limiting the resolution
of the machine [4].

00:05:48.410 --> 00:05:53.520
Electromagnets can be used to reach stronger
magnetic fields, but standard electromagnets

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:56.689
can’t produce a 1.5 tesla field.

00:05:56.689 --> 00:06:02.650
Higher magnetic fields require higher electric
currents that would melt ordinary wires.

00:06:02.650 --> 00:06:08.919
To achieve larger currents in the wires, engineers
required superconducting coils. [4]

00:06:08.919 --> 00:06:11.940
Superconductors are another sci-fi technology.

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:14.410
Temperature affects all metallic conductors.

00:06:14.410 --> 00:06:17.349
With resistance gradually lowering with temperature.

00:06:17.349 --> 00:06:22.449
But superconducting materials are special,
in that their resistance drops to zero at

00:06:22.449 --> 00:06:28.330
temperatures close to minus 273 degrees Celsius,
or absolute zero.

00:06:28.330 --> 00:06:34.170
In theory, when this happens, an electric
current could travel in a loop of superconducting

00:06:34.170 --> 00:06:37.630
material indefinitely, never needing a power
source.

00:06:37.630 --> 00:06:44.900
In reality, this means that the main superconducting
coil in MRIs does not consume any power directly.

00:06:44.900 --> 00:06:50.360
Rather, the main consumption of energy is
just to keep the coil cooled down so the current

00:06:50.360 --> 00:06:54.810
will travel endlessly, leaving the MRI magnet
permanently on.

00:06:54.810 --> 00:07:01.479
The energy needed to run an MRI for a full
year is equivalent to 25 four-person households,

00:07:01.479 --> 00:07:06.450
around 130,000 to 140,000 kWh per year.

00:07:06.450 --> 00:07:07.450
[5]

00:07:07.450 --> 00:07:10.620
The most common superconducting material used
in MRIs is Niobium–titanium.

00:07:10.620 --> 00:07:11.620
[6].

00:07:11.620 --> 00:07:18.650
The demand for High-resolution images is so
large that 80% of all the Nb-Ti we extract

00:07:18.650 --> 00:07:21.880
from the earth goes into an MRI machine [7].

00:07:21.880 --> 00:07:27.250
To achieve the incredibly low temperatures
needed for superconductivity, we need a very

00:07:27.250 --> 00:07:29.060
cold refrigerant.

00:07:29.060 --> 00:07:34.400
Early MRI machines used to submerge their
superconducting wires in a bath of liquid

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:35.400
Helium.

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:40.590
Pouring one thousand (1000) liters of liquid
helium, at minus 269 degrees celsius, into

00:07:40.590 --> 00:07:46.569
the machine to cool the superconducting coil
as close to absolute zero as possible.

00:07:46.569 --> 00:07:50.919
This evaporated the helium, allowing it to
escape the machine as a gas.

00:07:50.919 --> 00:07:56.600
Meaning, early MRI machines required regular
refillings of liquid helium.[8]

00:07:56.600 --> 00:08:00.860
Even though helium is an incredibly common
gas in the universe, it is so light that it

00:08:00.860 --> 00:08:03.669
can escape our atmosphere into space.

00:08:03.669 --> 00:08:08.979
We extract helium from underground gas caverns,
where it accumulated as a byproduct of uranium

00:08:08.979 --> 00:08:11.230
and thorium radioactive decay.

00:08:11.230 --> 00:08:16.030
But once we allow that helium to escape into
the atmosphere, it is gone for good.

00:08:16.030 --> 00:08:20.530
Floating to the top of our atmosphere and
gradually being blown into space by solar

00:08:20.530 --> 00:08:21.530
winds.

00:08:21.530 --> 00:08:24.500
We will eventually run out of natural helium.

00:08:24.500 --> 00:08:31.780
This method of cooling was costly and unsustainable,
costing up to $26,000 per year in helium refills.[9]

00:08:31.780 --> 00:08:37.130
To avoid this refilling problem, modern MRI
machines use a vacuum-sealed chamber that

00:08:37.130 --> 00:08:40.830
holds the liquid helium without letting it
evaporate.

00:08:40.830 --> 00:08:45.270
This eliminates the need for refilling and
minimizes the cost of operation.

00:08:45.270 --> 00:08:50.300
These so-called “Zero Boil off” machines
are now the norm in MRI technology.

00:08:50.300 --> 00:08:55.670
They use an electric refrigerant cycle like
the one in your fridge but on steroids.

00:08:55.670 --> 00:09:00.280
This cycle keeps the helium in its liquid
phase and keeps the magnets cool enough to

00:09:00.280 --> 00:09:03.649
maintain them in their superconducting state
[8].

00:09:03.649 --> 00:09:08.220
Now that we know why and how the hydrogen
atoms are aligned, we need a way to turn that

00:09:08.220 --> 00:09:13.110
information into an image, and to do that
we need to know the physical location of the

00:09:13.110 --> 00:09:14.810
hydrogen atoms.

00:09:14.810 --> 00:09:19.980
Recall that MRIs detect signals from the spiral
decay pattern of hydrogen atoms after they

00:09:19.980 --> 00:09:21.000
have been “nudged”.

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:26.180
These spirals decay with a unique rotational
frequency (⍵),. interestingly, this is the

00:09:26.180 --> 00:09:30.050
only frequency that can “nudge” the hydrogen
atoms too.

00:09:30.050 --> 00:09:34.820
Luckily, rotational frequency is dependent
on something we control, the magnetic field

00:09:34.820 --> 00:09:35.970
strength.

00:09:35.970 --> 00:09:44.560
For example, hydrogen resonates at 64 MHz
at 1.5 Tesla and at 128 MHz at 3 tesla[10].This

00:09:44.560 --> 00:09:49.430
means the atoms in a weaker magnetic field
will rotate slower while atoms in a stronger

00:09:49.430 --> 00:09:51.690
field will rotate faster.

00:09:51.690 --> 00:09:59.670
To nudge these atoms we need a radiofrequency
wave at 64 MHz or 128 MHz.

00:09:59.670 --> 00:10:04.310
We can use this to our advantage to image
individual slices.

00:10:04.310 --> 00:10:09.350
If we can apply a gradient to the magnetic
field strength, we can selectively nudge atoms

00:10:09.350 --> 00:10:13.279
along the gradient by applying the corresponding
frequency.

00:10:13.279 --> 00:10:18.769
This gradient is applied precisely using a
separate set of regular electromagnets, aptly

00:10:18.769 --> 00:10:20.519
named gradient coils.

00:10:20.519 --> 00:10:25.709
To image a slice near the weaker end of the
tube, the machine sends a “nudging pulse”

00:10:25.709 --> 00:10:33.310
centered at a lower rotational frequency say
63.998 MHz and to image a slice on the other

00:10:33.310 --> 00:10:38.690
side of the tube the pulse is centered at
64.002MHz.

00:10:38.690 --> 00:10:40.019
This still feels like magic.

00:10:40.019 --> 00:10:45.440
It’s not terribly obvious how detecting
the decay from these spiraling hydrogen atoms

00:10:45.440 --> 00:10:47.160
can create images.

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:52.510
The receiving coil can only measure the sum
of all these spiraling decays.

00:10:52.510 --> 00:10:56.470
We need a way of processing the signal to
create an image.

00:10:56.470 --> 00:11:01.720
Let's start by understanding how to get contrast
between tissues before delving deeper into

00:11:01.720 --> 00:11:05.339
the techniques used to actually form images.

00:11:05.339 --> 00:11:11.000
We need a way of identifying different kinds
of tissues to form an image, to do that we

00:11:11.000 --> 00:11:15.470
need to contrast the tissues using two different
signal types.

00:11:15.470 --> 00:11:20.820
The first one deals with how quickly atoms
re-align themselves with the large magnetic

00:11:20.820 --> 00:11:23.070
field after a nudging pulse.

00:11:23.070 --> 00:11:25.810
This is called T1 relaxation.

00:11:25.810 --> 00:11:30.840
The second measure comes from the physical
reality of interaction between hydrogens.

00:11:30.840 --> 00:11:34.970
The atoms do not realign with the magnetic
field uniformly.

00:11:34.970 --> 00:11:39.480
In tissue, hydrogens interact with each other
and with their surroundings.

00:11:39.480 --> 00:11:44.750
Right after the nudging pulse is sent, the
small interactions cause the spins to fall

00:11:44.750 --> 00:11:46.480
out of uniformity.

00:11:46.480 --> 00:11:51.810
Since the coil can only measure the sum of
all these spiral decays, then the failing

00:11:51.810 --> 00:11:54.959
out of order would create a decaying signal.

00:11:54.959 --> 00:11:57.190
This is called T2 decay.

00:11:57.190 --> 00:12:02.380
Importantly, the two rates are not equal,
and even more important they are dependent

00:12:02.380 --> 00:12:04.060
on the tissue.

00:12:04.060 --> 00:12:08.860
Hydrogens in fat have different intrinsic
characteristics and interactions than hydrogens

00:12:08.860 --> 00:12:09.980
in water.

00:12:09.980 --> 00:12:13.790
This difference is what lets technicians contrast
the tissues.[10]

00:12:13.790 --> 00:12:18.541
We can emphasize the T1 signal by sending
pulses rapidly and listening to the signal

00:12:18.541 --> 00:12:24.279
immediately, as the dephasing effects of T2
do not have enough time to take place.

00:12:24.279 --> 00:12:29.930
We can emphasize T2 by sending pulses slowly
and listening for longer, allowing the dephasing

00:12:29.930 --> 00:12:30.930
to occur.

00:12:30.930 --> 00:12:31.930
[11]

00:12:31.930 --> 00:12:35.389
Just like a photographer can play with camera
settings to take pictures of either the bright

00:12:35.389 --> 00:12:37.720
sky or a dim environment.

00:12:37.720 --> 00:12:43.540
MRI technicians can also play with two settings
to take images of contrasting tissues, the

00:12:43.540 --> 00:12:48.470
time between pulse repetition, and how long
to wait to listen for a signal.

00:12:48.470 --> 00:12:53.709
These two parameters are chosen by the technician
each time an MRI is performed and they choose

00:12:53.709 --> 00:12:56.730
them depending on what the doctor would like
to image.[10]

00:12:56.730 --> 00:13:02.500
For example, T1 is used to image fatty tissues
while suppressing the signal from water.

00:13:02.500 --> 00:13:07.899
But maybe the doctor wants to assess the cerebral
spinal fluid in the spine or the brain, and

00:13:07.899 --> 00:13:13.330
for that T2 signals are emphasized to enhance
the signal from water-based fluids.[12]

00:13:13.330 --> 00:13:18.820
Remember, the signal is collected as a sum
from a singular slice, The machine needs to

00:13:18.820 --> 00:13:22.139
form a 2D image of each slice.

00:13:22.139 --> 00:13:27.380
There is one extra layer of complexity since
the receiver coil can only measure the sum

00:13:27.380 --> 00:13:31.920
of the signals from all the decaying hydrogens
in the slice.

00:13:31.920 --> 00:13:37.820
This is yet another story where old mathematics
discoveries guide modern technology.

00:13:37.820 --> 00:13:44.000
In 1822 Joseph Fourier created a mathematical
framework that deconstructs complex waves

00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:46.920
into simple additions of simpler waves.

00:13:46.920 --> 00:13:51.160
This framework can be extended to 2D grayscale
geometries.

00:13:51.160 --> 00:13:55.920
Just like a musical melody can be simplified
into additions of simpler notes.

00:13:55.920 --> 00:14:01.690
Any image can be deconstructed into a weighted
average of simpler black and white stripes.

00:14:01.690 --> 00:14:04.560
This is what MRIS uses to create images.

00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:09.720
Instead of sampling individual pixels, MRIs
sample different striped patterns.

00:14:09.720 --> 00:14:13.140
In reality, what does this mean for the spinning
hydrogens?

00:14:13.140 --> 00:14:16.480
What is the physical representation of these
patterns?

00:14:16.480 --> 00:14:21.140
It is easier if we imagine the slice as a
grid of rotating hydrogens where we color

00:14:21.140 --> 00:14:23.800
the phase of the atoms in grey scale.

00:14:23.800 --> 00:14:27.560
When rotating in unison the grid is all white.

00:14:27.560 --> 00:14:31.850
Atoms rotating 180 degrees out of phase are
colored black.

00:14:31.850 --> 00:14:38.339
MRIs exploit this to physically create the
striped patterns needed for the Fourier analysis.

00:14:38.339 --> 00:14:44.200
Using another set of gradient coils MRIs precisely
change the phase of rotating hydrogens to

00:14:44.200 --> 00:14:46.389
create striped patterns.

00:14:46.389 --> 00:14:51.870
By controlling how long the Y and the X gradients
are turned on, the MRI can create patterns

00:14:51.870 --> 00:14:54.690
in all directions and frequencies.

00:14:54.690 --> 00:14:59.870
Slowly the machines start to imprint all the
patterns with different frequencies and orientations

00:14:59.870 --> 00:15:02.889
to sample their relative strengths in physical
space.

00:15:02.889 --> 00:15:08.139
And slowly by adding more and more patterns
the image starts to emerge.

00:15:08.139 --> 00:15:11.690
This is what forms the image for each 2D slice.

00:15:11.690 --> 00:15:16.320
Then the machine goes on to the next slice
of the body and starts the process all over

00:15:16.320 --> 00:15:17.500
again.

00:15:17.500 --> 00:15:22.130
Turning on and off coils inside a magnetic
field might sound familiar if you’ve ever

00:15:22.130 --> 00:15:23.560
played with a speaker.

00:15:23.560 --> 00:15:26.199
As basically they are the same things.

00:15:26.199 --> 00:15:30.970
Gradient coils rapidly turning on and off
are the source of the loud clunking sounds

00:15:30.970 --> 00:15:32.330
in MRIs.

00:15:32.330 --> 00:15:37.970
MRIs are constantly evolving and the core
technology is starting to branch off.

00:15:37.970 --> 00:15:42.550
Researchers want the highest resolution and
strive to increase the strength of the magnet,

00:15:42.550 --> 00:15:46.940
while some hospitals and companies are making
them smaller and cheaper, as more practical

00:15:46.940 --> 00:15:50.230
and cheap MRIS are incredibly useful in the
field.

00:15:50.230 --> 00:15:51.230
[13]

00:15:51.230 --> 00:15:54.959
It's hard to fathom the complexity of all
these systems, from superconducting wires

00:15:54.959 --> 00:16:00.420
to vacuum-sealed helium reserves to rapidly
changing magnetic gradients, and even more

00:16:00.420 --> 00:16:05.680
incredible to think of the first people who
figured out how to combine these technologies

00:16:05.680 --> 00:16:08.470
together to peer into our bodies.

00:16:08.470 --> 00:16:13.240
This intricate dance of quantum physics and
carefully manipulated gradients has allowed

00:16:13.240 --> 00:16:16.750
MRIs to change the world of medicine.

00:16:16.750 --> 00:16:21.490
The MRI machine is a truly astounding piece
of electronic technology.

00:16:21.490 --> 00:16:28.730
Developing something this complicated requires
the very best test and measurement equipment.

00:16:28.730 --> 00:16:33.970
Keysight puts the very best electronic measurement
and testing tools into engineers hands and

00:16:33.970 --> 00:16:38.420
are holding another virtual event for engineers
and on May 16th.

00:16:38.420 --> 00:16:43.930
This fantastic free event is a rare opportunity
to learn about electronics from experts.

